  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "The perimeter of a semicircular region, measured in centimeters, is numerically equal to its area, measured in square centimeters. The radius of the semicircle, measured in centimeters, is",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\pi\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac2\\pi\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac12\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac4\\pi+2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Let \\(r>0\\) be the radius measured in centimeters. The perimeter includes both the semicircular arc and the diameter, so its numerical value is \\(\\pi r+2r\\). The numerical area is half the area of the full circle, or \\(\\pi r^2/2\\).\n\nThe given equality of numerical measures therefore gives\n\\[\\pi r+2r=\\frac{\\pi r^2}{2}.\\]\nDividing by \\(r>0\\) removes the common radius factor and leaves \\(\\pi+2=\\pi r/2\\). Hence\n\\[r=\\frac{2(\\pi+2)}{\\pi}=2+\\frac4\\pi.\\]\nThe radius is \\(2+4/\\pi\\) centimeters, choice E.",
